# Former Gods, Part 5
Tamil title: மாஜி கடவுள்கள் - பகுதி 5

...went away to his work. The goddess raised her son, often telling him: “Your father is no ordinary man. He is Apollo, the handsome and mighty one admired by heaven and earth.” The boy Phaethon grew proud.

Another divine youth mocked him and demanded proof that Apollo was his father. Distressed and angry, Phaethon appealed to his mother, who sent him to Apollo. He journeyed to the god’s dazzling golden palace, where the chariot stood ready. Apollo welcomed him and publicly declared him his son.

The impetuous Phaethon asked to drive Apollo’s chariot for one day as proof. Apollo warned that no one else could control its horses, but he had already sworn by the Styx to grant any request. Breaking such an oath meant drinking its water, becoming a fool for a year, and being expelled from heaven for nine. He therefore instructed the boy carefully and yielded.

For a while the chariot followed its path; then the horses became a storm beyond Phaethon’s control. Moon and stars fled. The sun-chariot approached earth, seas dried, rivers became sand, forests burned, and people blackened. Hearing the world’s lament, Zeus awoke, understood at once, and killed Phaethon with a thunderbolt to save the universe. One must not ask what happened to the chariot: questions are marks of sin in mythology.

Many fruits of Apollo’s love likewise died in the bud. The maiden Clytie loved him without return and stared always toward his chariot. The gods transformed her into a sunflower, explaining why that flower faces the sun. Apollo was also a musician and cursed King Midas with donkey’s ears for saying another musician surpassed him.

This “solar mythology” remained sacred to Greeks for ages. They built enormous temples, conducted magnificent worship, and composed abundant poetry until new knowledge burned priestly falsehood and Apollo became a former god.

Apollo’s twin sister Artemis was the moon goddess. Beautiful enough to make heaven sigh, she obtained from Zeus a boon to remain a maiden.

## Artemis

Latona’s twins were Apollo and Artemis. He rode by day giving sunlight; she rode at night giving moonlight. Their mother’s joy became pride. When Niobe mocked her for having only two children while she had fourteen, Latona ordered Apollo and Artemis to kill them, especially after Niobe called their worship unnecessary.

Apollo killed the sons and Artemis the daughters. The youngest clung terrified to Niobe, who begged that one child be spared. Did the other gods intervene? No. They turned mother and child to stone. Artemis, who committed murder at her mother’s command, nevertheless supplied cool moonlight.

Her vow of maidenhood did not prevent love. Seeing a handsome shepherd named Endymion asleep below, she descended, kissed him, and vanished. Zeus, fearing his boon would fail, offered Endymion death or eternal sleep. He chose sleep and remained in a cave on Mount Latmus. Artemis visited at night, but he never awoke.

She later loved Orion, a hunter who had pursued seven forest nymphs. Artemis changed them first into creatures and then stars. Orion also tried to abduct a king’s daughter, was blinded, and recovered sight through the sun. When Artemis befriended him, Apollo opposed the match. Pointing to a dark object in the sea, he challenged her archery. She shot it, only to discover she had killed the swimming Orion. Grieving, she made him a star.

Another youth, Actaeon, secretly watched Artemis and nymphs bathing. She cursed him into a stag while leaving his human mind intact. His own hunting dogs pursued and tore him apart. Such were Artemis’s stories.

She enjoyed enormous influence, temples, and splendid worship while people believed these inventions. Then Artemis too became a former god.

One day Zeus suffered an unbearable headache. No god could cure him. He ordered his son Hephaestus to split his skull with an axe. When he obeyed, Athena emerged, not as an infant but a fully adorned maiden.

## Athena

Jealousy, rivalry, treachery, cruelty, and lust for authority are vices humanity must discard. Yet mythologies everywhere portray divine acts arising from them. If stories of the creator contain conduct humans themselves abhor, how can their recitation refine ordinary life? Mythologists cared chiefly to make their chosen deity stronger than rivals, not whether erotic and violent tales damaged the idea of God.

Gods of every rank pursued beauties and destroyed opponents. Heaven appeared to lack even the restraints found naturally among tribal peoples, while still preaching that evil humans went to hell and the virtuous to heaven. Greek and Roman myth called heaven the Elysian fields and hell Tartarus. It described gamblers, drunkards, and libertines tortured there while commanding worship of gods who did the same things. People took ages to perceive the contradiction; doubters were tortured.

The stranger a myth, the greater its prestige. Our own country believed humans arose from Brahma’s face, shoulders, thighs, and feet, and even today defenders assign these symbols of intellect, courage, labour, and service. Those boasting that such imagination belongs only to us would faint before the wonders Greeks and Romans invented.

Consider the omnipotent Zeus suffering a headache like any human. Asking whether disease is mightier than God was forbidden. Some laugh at foreign tales while claiming ours contain moral instruction, yet our rituals preserve similar beliefs: even now Ranganatha at Srirangam supposedly gets a headache and receives medicinal paste. Greece and Rome hold no festival to treat Zeus, because Zeus himself is gone; here treating Ranganatha’s headache remains devotion.

Unable to cure Zeus, the gods wrung their hands until Hephaestus split his skull. Athena emerged fully grown and the pain ceased. Divine physicians restored Zeus. Athena became goddess of defensive war, peace, and weaving. Greeks called her Athena, Romans Minerva; Athens itself bore her name. She stood beside Zeus as adviser and granted grace to defensive warriors.

Jealousy seized her when a mortal woman boasted no one in earth or heaven equalled her weaving. Disguised as an old woman, Athena warned her, but the woman challenged even the goddess. Both wove richly coloured, figured cloth. Realizing Athena would win, the woman hanged herself. Athena, unwilling to leave even the dead boastful woman alone, cursed her into a spider. Thus myth explained why spiders perpetually weave webs.

Lacking courage to reject the falsehood, people crowded temples for Athena’s grace. Ruins of her temple and its great statue remain at Athens, but her influence collapsed when true knowledge entered minds. No one there now explains spiders through Athena. We still point to three lines on a squirrel’s back and say the Lord stroked it. They learned not to build theology upon irrational stories and made Athena a former god.

Greeks and Romans worshipped Bacchus as the god who saved humanity by teaching the preparation of intoxicating drink. Crowds celebrated him magnificently, and his festival was reportedly more delightful than others.

## Bacchus, God of Wine

A handsome god rode a chariot drawn by panthers, surrounded by devotees intoxicated by the drink of his grace. Men and women sang and danced his praise. When devotion and liquor entered together, could their revelry be ordinary?

Today one asks whether a substance that destroys human dignity could be God’s gift. Yet for ages Greeks and Romans revered Bacchus, though only while they were gullible. He is now former. Those here who laugh forget that liquor is still offered in worship to Katteri, Madurai Veeran, and other deities.

Anyone comparing those ancient obscenities with rites continuing here in an advanced world would feel shame. Learned people supply philosophy for irrational worship and keep ordinary people in antiquity’s grip; therefore Katteri and Madurai Veeran remain while knowledge expelled Bacchus from Greece and Rome.

Bacchus was supposedly born from one of Zeus’s amorous conquests. Semele, beautiful daughter of Cadmus, king of Thebes, became his lover without human formalities. Zeus visited in human form but told her he was the supreme God.

Hera learned of the affair, disguised herself as a nurse, befriended Semele, and planted doubt. She insisted that an impostor was deceiving her and advised her to demand his divine form under oath. Semele followed the advice. Zeus warned that the sight would destroy her, but his prior oath bound him. When he revealed his radiant form, palace, possessions, and Semele burned to ash. Zeus saved only their unborn male child, Bacchus.

Thus runs the myth of his birth. Priests prohibited questions, rulers supported them, and nonsense became theism. Hera made further attempts to destroy Bacchus, but Zeus protected him. He taught humans to ferment grape juice and was worshipped for it. Festivals of drunken dancing spread; men and women drank in the name of the wine-god and...

---NOTES---
Part 5 begins in the middle of Apollo’s Phaethon narrative from `maji_kadavulgal4.md`. It ends mid-sentence after `என்று`, continued by `கூறிக் கொண்டு...` in `maji_kadavulgal6.md`; ellipses mark these inherited boundaries.

Conventional English forms are used for source spellings including `ப்யேடன்` (Phaethon), `கிளைட்டை` (Clytie), `ஆர்ட்டிமிஸ்` (Artemis), `நையோபி` (Niobe), `எண்டிமியான்` (Endymion), `ஓரியான்` (Orion), `ஆக்ட்டியான்` (Actaeon), `அதீனே` (Athena), `ஹீபாஸ்டஸ்` (Hephaestus), `பேகஸ்` (Bacchus), `காட்மஸ்` (Cadmus), and `செமிலி` (Semele). Tamil OCR remains unchanged.

The source’s `கோரியா நாடு` and its transformation of Orion’s seven nymphs contain uncertain or compressed readings. The translation follows the recoverable narrative without silently rewriting the Tamil.

The Athena weaving rival is unnamed in this source; the translation does not insert the conventional name Arachne.
